::ALG1_U03_L05_GP_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L05_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2
Question:
Write the equation of a line with slope 4 and y-intercept (0, -2).
{
~A. y \= -2x + 4#This reverses the slope and y-intercept.
=B. y \= 4x - 2#In y \= mx + b, m \= 4 and b \= -2.
~C. y \= 4x + 2#The slope is correct, but the y-intercept sign should be negative.
~D. y \= x + 2#This uses slope 1 and the wrong intercept sign.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L05_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2
Question:
A line has slope 3 and passes through (2, 11). What is its equation?
{
~A. y \= 3x + 11#This treats the y-value 11 as the y-intercept, but the point is not on the y-axis.
~B. y \= 2x + 11#This uses the x-coordinate as the slope.
~C. y \= 3x - 5#This subtracts instead of solving 11 \= 3(2) + b.
=D. y \= 3x + 5#11 \= 6 + b, so b \= 5.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L05_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.5
Question:
Which equation is parallel to y \= -2x + 6 and passes through (0, 5)?
{
=A. y \= -2x + 5#Parallel lines have the same slope, and the y-intercept is 5.
~B. y \= 2x + 5#This uses the opposite slope, not the same slope.
~C. y \= -2x + 6#This is the given line, not the new line through (0, 5).
~D. y \= 5x - 2#This reverses the point and slope information.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L05_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.5
Question:
A line is perpendicular to a line with slope 1/3 and passes through (0, 4). What is its equation?
{
~A. y \= 1/3x + 4#This is parallel because it keeps the same slope.
~B. y \= 3x + 4#This uses the reciprocal but does not change the sign.
=C. y \= -3x + 4#A perpendicular slope is the negative reciprocal of 1/3.
~D. y \= -1/3x + 4#This changes the sign but does not use the reciprocal.
}

::ALG1_U03_L05_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U03_L05_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.LR.2
Question:
A subscription costs $15 to start plus $6 per month. Which equation models total cost y after x months?
{
~A. y \= 15x + 6#This reverses the starting cost and monthly rate.
=B. y \= 6x + 15#The rate is 6 per month and the starting cost is 15.
~C. y \= 21x#This treats the sum of the rate and start cost as a single rate.
~D. y \= x + 21#This uses a rate of 1 instead of 6.
}

